"Orbit: a hydrogen electron in Bohr's model (OpenStax Vol. {#} §{#}) with the nucleus taken as infinitely heavy: radius n²a₀, speed αc/n. Passing any point once per period, it makes a current I = e/T round the loop; the loop's moment is μ = IA, and μ/L = e/(2m_e) for every orbit, so μ = n μ_B (§{#}). The quantum picture beside it uses the same n with any allowed l and m_l: |L| = √(l(l+{#})) ħ, L_z = m_l ħ, μ_z = −m_l μ_B, and in a field B the energy shift U = m_l μ_B B. Spin is left out in this view. L is drawn precessing about B; the real precession, at the Larmor frequency eB/({#}πm_e) ({#} GHz at {#} T), is far too fast to see, so the picture turns slowly.",
"Stern–Gerlach: atoms leave an oven through two slits (a ribbon {#} mm high and {#} mm long), cross a magnet {#} cm long whose field strength changes steadily across the beam (gradient dB/dz, taken as the same everywhere in the gap), drift a chosen distance and stick to a glass screen. An atom with moment component μ_z feels a steady force μ_z dB/dz, so it lands at z = (μ_z G/M)(L₁/v²)(L₁/{#} + L₂). A hot oven fills every sublevel m_J equally, with μ_z = −g_J m_J μ_B and g_J from the Landé formula with the electron's g factor {#}. Speeds in an effusive beam carry weight v³ exp(−Mv²/2kT); the deposit peaks where v² = 6kT/M, which the readouts use as the spot center without a velocity selector. With the selector every atom has the chosen speed. Not modeled: the field's change along the slit (which gave the {#} spots their lip shape), collisions, gravity (a fall of a few micrometers at most at a {#} cm drift, the same for every spot) and the nucleus's own moment (about {#} times smaller). The two imagined atoms are for comparison: an l = {#} atom with no spin (three spots), and classical magnets of moment μ_B pointing every way (one smeared band).",
"Zeeman effect: each level shifts by g m μ_B B in a field B. A component appears for every pair of upper and lower sublevels with Δm = {#} (π light) or ±{#} (σ light), with relative strength from the square of a 3j symbol. Across the field π components count fully and σ half; along the field π components are absent. With g = {#} in both levels (cadmium's singlet line, or hydrogen with spin left out as in §{#}) every component falls on one of three wavelengths, λ₀ and λ₀ ± λ²μ_B B/(hc): the normal Zeeman effect. The sodium D lines, whose levels have spin, split into four and six components (the anomalous Zeeman effect). g values come from the Landé formula with the electron's g factor and g_L = {#}. The spectrometer gives each component a Gaussian shape {#} pm wide (FWHM), finer than a real grating spectrometer; Zeeman splittings are measured with a Fabry–Perot étalon or a Lummer plate. Doppler width, hyperfine structure and isotope shifts are left out, and the field is uniform over the lamp.",
